        "## Questions\n",
        "\n",
        "Answer each question in the cell below it, in a few sentences, and support your answer with numbers from your own run.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Question 1. State design.** The state models the center with constant velocity and the size with a random walk. Why is it reasonable to leave the size without a velocity term on these videos? Name one situation in the benchmark where a constant-velocity model on the center is wrong, and say what the filter does in that situation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "cell-42",
      "metadata": {
        "papermill": {
          "duration": 0.093466,
          "end_time": "2026-10-26T11:34:36.364100+00:00",
          "exception": false,
          "start_time": "2026-10-26T11:34:36.270634+00:00",
          "status": "completed"
        },
        "tags": [],
        "id": "cell-42"
      },
      "source": [
        "It is reasonable to model the drone's size with a random walk because its bounding-box width and height do not usually change at a constant rate. Changes in size can result from perspective, distance, or detector noise, so adding velocity terms for width and height could make the model unnecessarily complicated.\n",
        "\n",
        "One situation where the constant-velocity assumption becomes inaccurate is when a drone suddenly changes direction or passes behind an obstacle. The Kalman filter initially predicts its next position using the previous velocity. When a valid detection becomes available, it corrects the prediction through a measurement update. If no detection passes the gate, the filter continues predicting temporarily before declaring the track lost."
      ]
    },
