      "source": [
        "When `accel_std` is much smaller than `meas_std`, the Kalman filter has a lower gain and trusts its motion predictions more than the detector. This helps reduce jitter from noisy detections but can cause the tracker to lag behind a fast-moving drone that suddenly changes direction. In my sweep, `accel_std=1` and `meas_std=30` produced an AUC of 0.629 and P@20 of 0.840.\n",
        "\n",
        "When `accel_std` is much larger than `meas_std`, the Kalman gain increases, making the filter trust detector measurements more. This allows it to respond faster to sudden movements but can also introduce errors from jittery detections. For example, `accel_std=25` and `meas_std=3` produced an AUC of 0.630 and P@20 of 0.838.\n",
        "\n",
        "The scores changed only slightly across the nine configurations, ranging from approximately 0.629 to 0.632 AUC. This is partly because my final tracker relies heavily on detector measurements when producing bounding boxes. Based on the odd-numbered sequences, the selected noise settings were `accel_std=6` and `meas_std=10`, with an overall AUC of approximately 0.631."
